lg x. So, there must be exactly one crossing in (2\u03c0, 5\u03c0/2). Then, between x=5\u03c0/2 and 3\u03c0, sin x decreases from 1 to 0, while lg x increases from ~0.895 to ~0.974. So, as sin x decreases and lg x increases, they cross again once. So that would be two crossings in (2\u03c0, 3\u03c0): one when sin x is increasing and crosses up, and another when sin x is decreasing and crosses down. Wait, no. Wait, in the interval (2\u03c0, 5\u03c0/2), sin x increases from 0 to 1, crosses lg x once. Then from 5\u03c0/2 to 3\u03c0, sin x decreases from 1 to 0, while lg x continues increasing. So, in that decreasing phase, since sin x starts at 1 above lg x (~0.895) and ends at 0 below lg x (~0.974), there must be another crossing. So that's two crossings in (2\u03c0, 3\u03c0).\n\nWait, that's two roots in that interval. Then previously, in (1, \u03c0), we had one root. So total three roots. But wait, the options don't have 3 as the answer unless C is correct. But the options are A.1, B.2, C.3, D. Greater than 3. Let me check again.\n\nWait, let me step back.\n\nFirst interval (0.1, \u03c0):\n\nWe identified one root in (1, \u03c0). But is there another root between (0.1, 1)?\n\nAt x=0.1, sin x\u22480.0998, lg x=-1. So sin x > lg x.\n\nAt x=1, sin x\u22480.8415, lg x=0. So sin x > lg x here. So in (0.1,1), both sin x and lg x are increasing. But since at both ends sin x is above lg x, and lg x is increasing faster? Wait, lg x from x=0.1 to 1 goes from -1 to 0, which is an increase of 1 over 0.9 units. While sin x increases from ~0.0998 to ~0.8415, which is an increase of ~0.74 over the same interval. So sin x is increasing more steeply than lg x here? Hmm. Wait, the derivative of sin x is cos x. At x=0.1, cos(0.1)\u22480.995, so the slope is ~0.995. The derivative of lg x is 1/(x ln 10). At x=0.1, derivative is 1/(0.1 * 2.3026)\u22484.34. So actually, lg x is increasing much faster in terms of slope at x=0.1, but since it starts from -1, maybe there's a crossing.\n\nWait, but at x=0.1, sin x\u22480.0998, lg x=-1. So sin x is way above. At x=1, sin x\u22480.8415, lg x=0. So sin x is still above. If both functions are increasing, but sin x starts higher and ends higher, is there a crossing in between? If the function that is initially above stays above, then no. But if the lower function (lg x) overtakes the upper one (sin x), then there would be a crossing. But since sin x is also increasing, we need to see if lg x catches up.\n\nWait, let's check an intermediate point in (0.1,1). Let's pick x=0.5.\n\nAt x=0.5: sin(0.5)\u22480.4794, lg 0.5\u2248-0.3010. So sin x\u22480.4794 > lg x\u2248-0.3010.\n\nAnother point, x=0.2:\n\nsin(0.2)\u22480.1987, lg 0.2\u2248-0.69897. Still sin x > lg x.\n\nx=0.3: sin\u22480.2955, lg\u2248-0.5229.\n\nx=0.4: sin\u22480.3894, lg\u2248-0.3979.\n\nx=0.6: sin\u22480.5646, lg\u2248-0.2218.\n\nSo in all these points, sin x is still greater than lg x. Since both are increasing, but sin x starts higher and remains higher, there is no crossing in (0.1,1). Therefore, only one root in (1, \u03c0).\n\nThen, moving to (\u03c0, 2\u03c0): as established, sin x is negative here, lg x is positive, so no roots.\n\nIn (2\u03c0, 3\u03c0):\n\nEarlier analysis suggests that there might be two crossings here. Let's check again.\n\nFrom x=2\u03c0 (6.28) to x=5\u03c0/2 (7.85), sin x increases from 0 to 1. lg x increases from ~0.798 to ~0.895. At x=2\u03c0, sin x=0 < lg x. At x=5\u03c0/2, sin x=1 > lg x. Since both are increasing, but sin x starts lower and ends higher, there must be exactly one crossing here.\n\nThen, from x=5\u03c0/2 (7.85) to x=3\u03c0 (9.42), sin x decreases from 1 to 0, while lg x increases from ~0.895 to ~0.974. At x=5\u03c0/2, sin x=1 > lg x\u22480.895. At x=3\u03c0, sin x=0 < lg x\u22480.974. Since sin x is decreasing and lg x is increasing, they must cross exactly once in this interval.\n\nThus, in (2\u03c0, 3\u03c0), two crossings. Therefore, total roots so far: 1 (from (1,\u03c0)) + 2 (from (2\u03c0,3\u03c0)) = 3.\n\nThen, what about between 3\u03c0 and 10 (since 3\u03c0\u22489.42 and 10). In this interval, sin x is negative (since 3\u03c0 to 4\u03c0 is next, but 10 is less than 4\u03c0\u224812.56). At x=10, which is 10 radians, sin 10\u2248-0.5440. lg 10=1. So in this interval (9.42,10), sin x is negative, lg x is increasing from ~0.974 to 1. So sin x negative, lg x positive. No crossings here.\n\nSo total roots would be 3. So answer C.3.\n\nBut wait, let me verify with some approximate calculations.\n\nFirst root in (1, \u03c0):\n\nLet's take x=2: sin(2)\u22480.909, lg 2\u22480.301. So sin x > lg x.\n\nx=3: sin 3\u22480.141, lg 3\u22480.477. So sin x < lg x. So crossing between 2 and 3.\n\nUsing the Intermediate Value Theorem, yes, there's a root between 2 and 3.\n\nSecond root in (2\u03c0,5\u03c0/2):\n\nx=6.5: sin(6.5)=sin(6.5 - 2\u03c0)=sin(6.5 -6.28)=sin(0.22)\u22480.218. lg 6.5\u22480.812. So sin x < lg x.\n\nx=7: sin(7)=sin(7 -2\u03c0)=sin(0.72)\u22480.659. lg 7\u22480.845. Still sin x < lg x.\n\nx=7.5: sin(7.5)=sin(7.5 -2\u03c0)=sin(1.22)\u22480.939. lg 7.5\u22480.875. So sin x > lg x. Therefore, crossing between 7 and 7.5.\n\nThird root in (5\u03c0/2,3\u03c0):\n\nx=8: sin(8)=sin(8 -2\u03c0)=sin(1.72)\u22480.987. lg 8\u22480.903. sin x > lg x.\n\nx=9: sin(9)=sin(9 - 3\u03c0)=sin(9 -9.42)=sin(-0.42)= -sin(0.42)\u2248-0.407. lg 9\u22480.954. So here, sin x is negative, lg x positive, so no. Wait, but wait, 3\u03c0\u22489.42, so x=9 is before 3\u03c0. Let's recast.\n\nWait, x=8.5: sin(8.5)=sin(8.5 - 3\u03c0)=? Wait, 8.5 is between 2\u03c0 and 3\u03c0. Let me compute 8.5 radians.\n\nWait, 2\u03c0\u22486.28, so 8.5 - 2\u03c0\u22482.22 radians. sin(2.22)\u22480.8. lg 8.5\u22480.929. So sin x\u22480.8 < lg x\u22480.929.\n\nx=9: as above, sin x\u2248-0.407 < lg x\u22480.954.\n\nWait, this seems contradictory. Wait, maybe I made a mistake here.\n\nWait, when x is in (2\u03c0,3\u03c0), which is approximately (6.28,9.42). The function sin x in this interval can be represented as sin(x - 2\u03c0), which would be equivalent to sin y where y = x - 2\u03c0. So for x in (2\u03c0,5\u03c0/2), which is (6.28,7.85), y = x - 2\u03c0 is in (0, \u03c0/2), so sin y is positive and increasing. Then from x=5\u03c0/2 (7.85) to 3\u03c0 (9.42), y = x - 2\u03c0 is in (\u03c0/2, \u03c0), so sin y is positive but decreasing.\n\nWait, no. Actually, sin(x) for x in (2\u03c0, 3\u03c0) is the same as sin(x - 2\u03c0) because sin is 2\u03c0-periodic. So x - 2\u03c0 ranges from 0 to \u03c0 (since 3\u03c0 - 2\u03c0 = \u03c0). So sin(x) in (2\u03c0,3\u03c0) is sin(y) where y \u2208 (0,\u03c0). Therefore, sin(x) starts at 0, rises to 1 at y=\u03c0/2 (x=2\u03c0 + \u03c0/2=5\u03c0/2\u22487.85), then decreases back to 0 at y=\u03c0 (x=3\u03c0\u22489.42). Therefore, in (2\u03c0,3\u03c0), sin x first increases to 1 then decreases to 0.\n\nTherefore, in the interval (2\u03c0,5\u03c0/2)\u2248(6.28,7.85), sin x increases from 0 to 1. Then in (5\u03c0/2,3\u03c0)\u2248(7.85,9.42), sin x decreases from 1 to 0.\n\nSo, going back to x=8.5: x=8.5 is in (2\u03c0,3\u03c0). Specifically, x=8.5 -2\u03c0\u22488.5 -6.28\u22482.22 radians. sin(2.22)=sin(\u03c0 - 2.22)=sin(0.921)\u22480.799. So sin(8.5)=sin(2.22)=0.799. lg 8.5\u22480.929. So 0.799 < 0.929, so sin x < lg x.\n\nx=9: sin(9)=sin(9 -2\u03c0)=sin(9 -6.28)=sin(2.72). 2.72 radians is \u03c0 - 0.42 radians, so sin(2.72)=sin(\u03c0 -0.42)=sin(0.42)\u22480.407. But since it's in the second quadrant, sin is positive. Wait, but x=9 is 9 radians, which is 2\u03c0 + (9 - 6.28)=2\u03c0 +2.72. But 2.72 radians is greater than \u03c0 (3.14?), no. Wait, 2.72 radians is less than \u03c0\u22483.14. So sin(9)=sin(2\u03c0 +2.72)=sin(2.72). Since 2.72 is in the second quadrant (between \u03c0/2 and \u03c0), so sin(2.72)\u2248sin(\u03c0 - (\u03c0 -2.72))=sin(\u03c0 -0.42)=sin(0.42)=0.407, but wait, no. Wait, sin(\u03c0 - \u03b8)=sin\u03b8. So sin(2.72)=sin(\u03c0 -0.42)=sin(0.42)\u22480.407. So sin(9)\u22480.407. lg 9\u22480.954. So sin x=0.407 < lg x=0.954.\n\nAt x=3\u03c0\u22489.42, sin x=0, lg x\u22480.974.\n\nSo between x=5\u03c0/2 (7.85) and x=3\u03c0 (9.42), sin x decreases from 1 to 0, while lg x increases from ~0.895 to ~0.974. At x=7.85, sin x=1 > lg x\u22480.895. At x=9.42, sin x=0 < lg x\u22480.974. Therefore, there must be a crossing in this interval. For example, let's check x=8:\n\nsin(8)=sin(8 -2\u03c0)=sin(1.72)\u22480.987. lg 8\u22480.903. So sin x=0.987 > lg x=0.903.\n\nx=8.5: sin\u22480.799 < lg\u22480.929. So crossing between x=8 and 8.5. Therefore, one root here.\n\nSimilarly, between x=2\u03c0 (6.28) and x=5\u03c0/2 (7.85), sin x increases from 0 to 1, crossing lg x once. For example, at x=7, sin x\u22480.659 < lg x\u22480.845. At x=7.5, sin x\u22480.939 > lg x\u22480.875. So crossing between 7 and 7.5. Therefore, one root here.\n\nSo in total, in (2\u03c0,3\u03c0), two roots. Adding the one in (1, \u03c0), total three roots.\n\nBut let me check if there's another crossing in (0.1,1). Earlier, I didn't find any, but perhaps at very low x?\n\nWait, as x approaches 0.1 from the right, lg x approaches -1, and sin x approaches sin(0.1)\u22480.0998. So sin x is always above lg x in (0.1,1). Therefore, no crossings there.\n\nWhat about between x=3\u03c0 and 10? As x approaches 10, sin x is sin(10)\u2248-0.5440, lg x=1. So no crossings there.\n\nTherefore, total of three real roots: one in (1, \u03c0), one in (2\u03c0,5\u03c0/2), and one in (5\u03c0/2,3\u03c0). So answer C.3.\n\nBut wait, I should check if there's another root in (3\u03c0,10). sin x is negative there, lg x is positive, so no.\n\nWait, but let's confirm with another example. Let's check x=9.5:\n\nsin(9.5)=sin(9.5 - 3\u03c0)=sin(9.5 -9.42)=sin(0.08)\u22480.0798. lg 9.5\u22480.978. So sin x\u22480.0798 < lg x\u22480.978. Still, sin x < lg x.\n\nAt x=10: sin(10)=sin(10 -3\u03c0)=sin(10 -9.42)=sin(0.58)\u22480.547. Wait, no. Wait, 3\u03c0\u22489.42, so 10 radians is 10 - 3\u03c0\u22480.58 radians. So sin(10)=sin(3\u0