,3,5,7 is 216 numbers. Therefore, any subset larger than 216 must include at least one number not in this union. That number, along with 2,3,5,7, forms five pairwise coprimes. Hence, n=217.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \\boxed{217}.", "deepseek_solution": "To determine the smallest positive integer \\( n \\) such that every subset of \\( S = \\{1, 2, 3, \\ldots, 280\\} \\) with \\( n \\) elements contains 5 pairwise coprime numbers, we start by considering the complementary problem: finding the largest subset of \\( S \\) that does not contain 5 pairwise coprime numbers.\n\n1. **Prime Multiples Analysis**:\n   - The largest subsets that avoid pairwise coprime numbers are typically composed of multiples of a few primes.\n   - The four smallest primes (2, 3, 5, 7) have the most multiples in \\( S \\).\n\n2. **Inclusion-Exclusion Principle**:\n   - Calculate the number of elements in the union of multiples of 2, 3, 5, and 7 using the inclusion-exclusion principle.\n   - Results:\n     - \\( |A| = 140 \\), \\( |B| = 93 \\), \\( |C| = 56 \\), \\( |D| = 40 \\)\n     - Pairwise intersections: 46, 28, 20, 18, 13, 8\n     - Triple intersections: 9, 6, 4, 2\n     - Quadruple intersection: 1\n   - Total union size: \\( 140 + 93 + 56 + 40 - 46 - 28 - 20 - 18 - 13 - 8 + 9 + 6 + 4 + 2 - 1 = 216 \\)\n\n3. **Key Insight**:\n   - Any subset larger than 216 must include at least one number not divisible by 2, 3, 5, or 7.\n   - Such a number is coprime to 2, 3, 5, and 7. Including this number along with 2, 3, 5, and 7 forms a set of 5 pairwise coprime numbers.\n\n4. **Conclusion**:\n   - The largest subset without 5 pairwise coprime numbers has 216 elements.\n   - Therefore, the smallest \\( n \\) such that every subset of size \\( n \\) contains 5 pairwise coprime numbers is \\( 216 + 1 = 217 \\).\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{217}\n\\]", "ground_truth_solution": "\n1. **Step 1: Define the sets and their sizes**\n\n   Let \\( S = \\{1, 2, 3, \\ldots, 280\\} \\).\n\n   Define the subsets of \\( S \\) as follows:\n   \\begin{align*}\n   &A_1 = \\{ \\text{numbers in } S \\text{ divisible by } 2\\}, \\\\\n   &A_2 = \\{ \\text{numbers in } S \\text{ divisible by } 3 \\}, \\\\\n   &A_3 = \\{ \\text{numbers in } S \\text{ divisible by } 5 \\}, \\\\\n   &A_4 = \\{ \\text{numbers in } S \\text{ divisible by } 7 \\}. \n   \\end{align*}\n   \n   Let \\( A = A_1 \\cup A_2 \\cup A_3 \\cup A_4 \\).\n\n2. **Step 2: Use the Inclusion-exclusion principle to find the size of \\( A \\)**\n\n   \\[\n   \\begin{aligned}\n   |A| &= |A_1 \\cup A_2 \\cup A_3 \\cup A_4| \\\\\n       &= |A_1| + |A_2| + |A_3| + |A_4| - |A_1 \\cap A_2| - |A_1 \\cap A_3| \\\\\n       &\\quad - |A_1 \\cap A_4| - |A_2 \\cap A_3| - |A_2 \\cap A_4| - |A_3 \\cap A_4| \\\\\n       &\\quad + |A_1 \\cap A_2 \\cap A_3| + |A_1 \\cap A_2 \\cap A_4| + |A_1 \\cap A_3 \\cap A_4| \\\\\n       &\\quad + |A_2 \\cap A_3 \\cap A_4| - |A_1 \\cap A_2 \\cap A_3 \\cap A_4|.\n   \\end{aligned}\n   \\]\n\n3. **Step 3: Compute the individual intersection sizes**\n\n   \\[\n   \\begin{aligned}\n   &|A_1| = \\left\\lfloor \\frac{280}{2} \\right\\rfloor = 140, \\\\\n   &|A_2| = \\left\\lfloor \\frac{280}{3} \\right\\rfloor = 93, \\\\\n   &|A_3| = \\left\\lfloor \\frac{280}{5} \\right\\rfloor = 56, \\\\\n   &|A_4| = \\left\\lfloor \\frac{280}{7} \\right\\rfloor = 40.\n   \\end{aligned}\n   \\]\n\n   Next, for the pairwise intersections:\n   \\[\n   \\begin{aligned}\n   &|A_1 \\cap A_2| = \\left\\lfloor \\frac{280}{6} \\right\\rfloor = 46, \\\\\n   &|A_1 \\cap A_3| = \\left\\lfloor \\frac{280}{10} \\right\\rfloor = 28, \\\\\n   &|A_1 \\cap A_4| = \\left\\lfloor \\frac{280}{14} \\right\\rfloor = 20, \\\\\n   &|A_2 \\cap A_3| = \\left\\lfloor \\frac{280}{15} \\right\\rfloor = 18, \\\\\n   &|A_2 \\cap A_4| = \\left\\lfloor \\frac{280}{21} \\right\\rfloor = 13, \\\\\n   &|A_3 \\cap A_4| = \\left\\lfloor \\frac{280}{35} \\right\\rfloor = 8.\n   \\end{aligned}\n   \\]\n\n   For the triple intersections:\n   \\[\n   \\begin{aligned}\