e dimensions of the cone (height and base radius) such that the sphere of radius r is perfectly inscribed inside the cone. \n\nI recall that for a cone to have an inscribed sphere (which is called a tangent sphere), the sphere touches the cone along its lateral surface and the base. The formula for the radius of the inscribed sphere in a cone relates to the height h and the base radius R of the cone. Let me try to derive this.\n\nLet me imagine the cone with height h and base radius R. The sphere inside the cone will touch the cone's lateral surface and the base. The center of the sphere must lie along the central axis of the cone. The distance from the apex of the cone to the center of the sphere is h - r, since the sphere has radius r and touches the base of the cone, which is distance h from the apex.\n\nNow, consider a cross-sectional view of the cone and sphere. The cross-section is a triangle (the cone) with a circle inscribed in it. The triangle has a height h and base 2R. The inscribed circle (sphere's cross-section) has radius r and is tangent to the sides of the triangle and the base.\n\nIn this cross-section, the center of the circle is at a distance r from the base (since it touches the base) and at a distance h - r from the apex. The sides of the cone are lines from the apex (0, h) to the base points (R, 0) and (-R, 0). The slope of the side is (0 - h)/(R - 0) = -h/R. So the equation of the side is y = - (h/R)x + h.\n\nThe distance from the center of the circle (0, r) to the side of the cone must be equal to the radius r. The distance from a point (x0, y0) to the line ax + by + c = 0 is |ax0 + by0 + c| / sqrt(a\u00b2 + b\u00b2). Let's apply this.\n\nThe equation of the side is y = - (h/R)x + h. Let's rearrange this to standard form: (h/R)x + y - h = 0. So a = h/R, b = 1, c = -h.\n\nThe center of the circle is at (0, r). So the distance from (0, r) to the line is |(h/R)*0 + 1*r - h| / sqrt((h/R)\u00b2 + 1\u00b2) = |r - h| / sqrt((h\u00b2/R\u00b2) + 1). This distance must equal the radius r. Therefore:\n\n|r - h| / sqrt((h\u00b2/R\u00b2) + 1) = r\n\nSince h > r (the center is at height r, and the apex is at height h, so h must be greater than r), we can drop the absolute value:\n\n(h - r) / sqrt((h\u00b2/R\u00b2) + 1) = r\n\nMultiply both sides by the denominator:\n\nh - r = r * sqrt((h\u00b2/R\u00b2) + 1)\n\nSquare both sides to eliminate the square root:\n\n(h - r)^2 = r\u00b2 * ((h\u00b2/R\u00b2) + 1)\n\nExpand the left side:\n\nh\u00b2 - 2hr + r\u00b2 = r\u00b2*(h\u00b2/R\u00b2 + 1)\n\nDivide both sides by r\u00b2:\n\n(h\u00b2 - 2hr + r\u00b2)/r\u00b2 = h\u00b2/R\u00b2 + 1\n\nSimplify the left side:\n\n(h\u00b2/r\u00b2 - 2h/r + 1) = h\u00b2/R\u00b2 + 1\n\nSubtract 1 from both sides:\n\nh\u00b2/r\u00b2 - 2h/r = h\u00b2/R\u00b2\n\nMultiply both sides by R\u00b2:\n\nR\u00b2*(h\u00b2/r\u00b2 - 2h/r) = h\u00b2\n\nDivide both sides by h\u00b2 (assuming h \u2260 0):\n\nR\u00b2*(1/r\u00b2 - 2/(h r)) = 1\n\nLet me rearrange terms:\n\nR\u00b2 = h\u00b2 / (1/r\u00b2 - 2/(h r)) ?\n\nWait, let me check that again. Wait, starting from:\n\nh\u00b2/r\u00b2 - 2h/r = h\u00b2/R\u00b2\n\nLet me factor h\u00b2/R\u00b2:\n\nh\u00b2/R\u00b2 = h\u00b2/r\u00b2 - 2h/r\n\nBring all terms to one side:\n\nh\u00b2/R\u00b2 - h\u00b2/r\u00b2 + 2h/r = 0\n\nFactor h\u00b2:\n\nh\u00b2(1/R\u00b2 - 1/r\u00b2) + 2h/r = 0\n\nHmm, maybe this approach is getting too messy. Let me try another way.\n\nAlternatively, let me denote the half-angle at the apex of the cone as \u03b8. Then, in the cross-sectional view, the triangle has a height h and base radius R, so tan \u03b8 = R / h.\n\nThe inscribed circle (radius r) is tangent to the sides of the triangle. The distance from the apex to the center of the circle is h - r. The center is located at (0, r) in the coordinate system where the apex is at (0, h) and the base is at y=0.\n\nThe distance from the center to the side of the cone is equal to r. Using trigonometry, the distance from the center to the side can be related to the angle \u03b8.\n\nIn the triangle formed by the apex, the center of the circle, and the point where the radius meets the side of the cone, we can form a right triangle. The distance from the center to the side is r, the distance from the apex to the center is h - r, and the angle at the apex is \u03b8.\n\nTherefore, sin \u03b8 = opposite / hypotenuse = r / (h - r)\n\nBut tan \u03b8 = R / h, so sin \u03b8 = R / sqrt(R\u00b2 + h\u00b2)\n\nTherefore:\n\nR / sqrt(R\u00b2 + h\u00b2) = r / (h - r)\n\nCross-multiplying:\n\nR (h - r) = r sqrt(R\u00b2 + h\u00b2)\n\nSquare both sides:\n\nR\u00b2 (h - r)^2 = r\u00b2 (R\u00b2 + h\u00b2)\n\nExpand the left side:\n\nR\u00b2 (h\u00b2 - 2hr + r\u00b2) = r\u00b2 R\u00b2 + r\u00b2 h\u00b2\n\nLeft side: R\u00b2 h\u00b2 - 2 R\u00b2 h r + R\u00b2 r\u00b2\n\nRight side: r\u00b2 R\u00b2 + r\u00b2 h\u00b2\n\nSubtract right side from left side:\n\nR\u00b2 h\u00b2 - 2 R\u00b2 h r + R\u00b2 r\u00b2 - r\u00b2 R\u00b2 - r\u00b2 h\u00b2 = 0\n\nSimplify:\n\nR\u00b2 h\u00b2 - 2 R\u00b2 h r - r\u00b2 h\u00b2 = 0\n\nFactor h\u00b2:\n\nh\u00b2 (R\u00b2 - r\u00b2) - 2 R\u00b2 h r = 0\n\nHmm, not sure if this is helpful. Let's factor h:\n\nh [h (R\u00b2 - r\u00b2) - 2 R\u00b2 r] = 0\n\nSince h \u2260 0, then:\n\nh (R\u00b2 - r\u00b2) - 2 R\u00b2 r = 0\n\nSolve for h:\n\nh (R\u00b2 - r\u00b2) = 2 R\u00b2 r\n\nh = (2 R\u00b2 r) / (R\u00b2 - r\u00b2)\n\nHmm, okay. So h is expressed in terms of R. Alternatively, we can write this as h = 2 R\u00b2 r / (R\u00b2 - r\u00b2)\n\nAlternatively, maybe express R in terms of h. Let me see. Let me denote k = R/h, so R = k h. Then substituting into h = 2 R\u00b2 r / (R\u00b2 - r\u00b2):\n\nh = 2 (k\u00b2 h\u00b2) r / (k\u00b2 h\u00b2 - r\u00b2)\n\nMultiply both sides by denominator:\n\nh (k\u00b2 h\u00b2 - r\u00b2) = 2 k\u00b2 h\u00b2 r\n\nDivide both sides by h (assuming h \u2260 0):\n\nk\u00b2 h\u00b2 - r\u00b2 = 2 k\u00b2 h r\n\nRearrange:\n\nk\u00b2 h\u00b2 - 2 k\u00b2 h r - r\u00b2 = 0\n\nThis is a quadratic equation in h:\n\nk\u00b2 h\u00b2 - 2 k\u00b2 r h - r\u00b2 = 0\n\nBut this might not be the most straightforward approach. Maybe instead, express R in terms of h from the earlier equation:\n\nFrom h = (2 R\u00b2 r)/(R\u00b2 - r\u00b2)\n\nMultiply both sides by (R\u00b2 - r\u00b2):\n\nh R\u00b2 - h r\u00b2 = 2 R\u00b2 r\n\nBring all terms to one side:\n\nh R\u00b2 - h r\u00b2 - 2 R\u00b2 r = 0\n\nFactor R\u00b2:\n\nR\u00b2 (h - 2 r) - h r\u00b2 = 0\n\nSolve for R\u00b2:\n\nR\u00b2 = (h r\u00b2)/(h - 2 r)\n\nTherefore, R = r sqrt( h / (h - 2 r) )\n\nThis expression requires that h > 2 r, otherwise the denominator becomes negative or zero, which isn't possible. So the height of the cone must be greater than 2r.\n\nNow, the volume of the cone is (1/3)\u03c0 R\u00b2 h. Substitute R\u00b2 from above:\n\nV2 = (1/3) \u03c0 * (h r\u00b2 / (h - 2 r)) * h = (1/3) \u03c0 r\u00b2 h\u00b2 / (h - 2 r)\n\nSo V2 = (\u03c0 r\u00b2 h\u00b2) / [3 (h - 2 r)]\n\nWe need to express V2 in terms of h and then find the value of h that minimizes the ratio V2 / V1.\n\nSince V1 is 2 \u03c0 r\u00b3, the ratio V2/V1 is:\n\n[(\u03c0 r\u00b2 h\u00b2)/(3 (h - 2 r))] / [2 \u03c0 r\u00b3] = [\u03c0 r\u00b2 h\u00b2] / [3 (h - 2 r) * 2 \u03c0 r\u00b3] = [h\u00b2] / [6 r (h - 2 r)]\n\nSo the ratio simplifies to h\u00b2 / [6 r (h - 2 r)]\n\nTherefore, to find the minimum of V2/V1, we need to minimize the function f(h) = h\u00b2 / [6 r (h - 2 r)] with respect to h, where h > 2 r.\n\nSince r is a constant, we can treat this as a function of h. Let me set f(h) = h\u00b2 / (6 r (h - 2 r)). Let me factor out the constants:\n\nf(h) = (1/(6 r)) * h\u00b2 / (h - 2 r)\n\nTo find the minimum, take the derivative of f(h) with respect to h, set it to zero, and solve for h.\n\nLet me compute f'(h):\n\nLet me denote f(h) = (1/(6 r)) * [ h\u00b2 / (h - 2 r) ]\n\nSo f'(h) = (1/(6 r)) * [ (2 h (h - 2 r) - h\u00b2 (1) ) / (h - 2 r)^2 ]\n\nSimplify the numerator inside the brackets:\n\n2 h (h - 2 r) - h\u00b2 = 2 h\u00b2 - 4 h r - h\u00b2 = h\u00b2 - 4 h r\n\nTherefore, f'(h) = (1/(6 r)) * (h\u00b2 - 4 h r) / (h - 2 r)^2\n\nSet derivative equal to zero:\n\n(h\u00b2 - 4 h r) / (h - 2 r)^2 = 0\n\nThe denominator is always positive for h > 2 r, so set numerator equal to zero:\n\nh\u00b2 - 4 h r = 0\n\nFactor:\n\nh (h - 4 r) = 0\n\nSolutions are h = 0 or h = 4 r. But h > 2 r, so the critical point is at h = 4 r.\n\nNow, we need to confirm that this critical point is indeed a minimum. Let's check the second derivative or test intervals around h = 4 r.\n\nAlternatively, consider the behavior of f(h):\n\nAs h approaches 2 r from above, the denominator (h - 2 r) approaches zero, so f(h) approaches infinity.\n\nAs h approaches infinity, f(h) \u2248 h\u00b2 / (6 r h) = h / (6 r), which also approaches infinity.\n\nTherefore, the function f(h) has a minimum somewhere between h = 2 r and h approaching infinity. The critical point at h = 4 r must be this minimum.\n\nTherefore, the minimal ratio occurs when h = 4 r.\n\nNow, substitute h = 4 r into the expression for R:\n\nFrom earlier, R = r sqrt( h / (h - 2 r) )\n\nPlug h = 4 r:\n\nR = r sqrt( (4 r) / (4 r - 2 r) ) = r sqrt(4 r / 2 r) = r sqrt(2) = r\u221a2\n\nTherefore, when h = 4 r, R = r\u221a2.\n\nNow, compute the ratio V2/V1 at h = 4 r:\n\nFrom the ratio expression f(h) = h\u00b2 / [6 r (h - 2 r)]\n\nSubstitute h = 4 r:\n\n( (4 r)^2 ) / [6 r (4 r - 2 r) ] = (16 r\u00b2) / [6 r * 2 r] = 16 r\u00b2 / 12 r\u00b2 = 16/12 = 4/3 \u2248 1.333...\n\nWait, that can't be. The minimal ratio is 4/3? That seems a bit counterintuitive, but let's check.\n\nAlternatively, compute V2 and V1 when h = 4 r.\n\nFirst, V1 = 2 \u03c0 r\u00b3.\n\nV2 is (1/3)\u03c0 R\u00b2 h. With R = r\u221a2 and h = 4 r:\n\nV2 = (1/3) \u03c0 ( (r\u221a2)^2 ) (4 r) = (1/3) \u03c0 (2 r\u00b2) (4 r) = (1/3) \u03c0 8 r\u00b3 = (8/3) \u03c0 r\u00b3\n\nTherefore, the ratio V2/V1 = (8/3 \u03c0 r\u00b3) / (2 \u03c0 r\u00b3) = (8/3)/2 = 4/3.\n\nSo the minimal ratio is indeed 4/3.\n\nBut wait, that seems to suggest that the minimal ratio is 4/3, which is approximately 1.333. However, intuitively, I might have expected a lower ratio, but given the calculations, that seems correct.\n\nLet me check the steps again to ensure there's no error.\n\n1. For the cylinder: sphere inscribed, so diameter 2r, height 2r. Volume V1 = \u03c0r\u00b2 * 2r = 2\u03c0r\u00b3. Correct.\n\n2. For the cone: derived the relation between h and R when a sphere of radius r is inscribed. Got to the expression h = 2 R\u00b2 r / (R\u00b2 - r\u00b2). Then expressed R in terms of h: R = r sqrt( h / (h - 2 r) ). Correct.\n\n3. Expressed V2 = (\u03c0 r\u00b2 h\u00b2) / [3 (h - 2 r)]. Then computed ratio V2/V1 as h\u00b2 / [6 r (h - 2 r)]. Correct.\n\n4. Took derivative, found critical point at h = 4r. Plugged back, got R = r\u221a2. Calculated V2 as (8/3) \u03c0 r\u00b3. Ratio 4/3. All steps seem correct.\n\nTherefore, the minimal ratio is indeed 4/3. So the minimum value of V2/V1 is 4/3.\n\nBut let me verify with another approach. Maybe using similar triangles.\n\nIn the cross-section, when the sphere is inscribed in the cone, the center of the sphere is at (0, r). The sphere touches the cone's lateral surface at some point. Let's consider the triangle formed by the apex of the cone (0, h), the center of the sphere (0, r), and the point where the sphere is tangent to the cone's lateral surface.\n\nThe tangent point lies on both the sphere and the cone. The sphere's equation is x\u00b2 + (y - r)^2 = r\u00b2. The cone's lateral surface equation is y = - (h/R) x + h.\n\nAt the point of tangency, these two equations meet, and the derivative (slope) of the sphere and the cone are equal.\n\nAlternatively, using geometry, the tangent line from the center of the sphere to the cone's lateral surface must be perpendicular to the cone's surface.\n\nBut maybe this is overcomplicating. Since the earlier calculus approach gave a consistent answer, I think it's safe.\n\nTherefore, the minimal ratio is 4/3.\n\n**Final Answer**\nThe minimum value of the ratio \\( \\dfrac{V_2}{V_1} \\) is \\boxed{\\dfrac{4}{3}}.", "deepseek_solution": "To find the minimum value of the ratio \\( \\dfrac{V_2}{V_1} \\), where \\( V_1 \\) is the volume of a cylinder circumscribed around a sphere with radius \\( r \\), and \\( V_2 \\) is the volume of a cone circumscribed around the same sphere, we proceed as follows:\n\n1. **Volume of the Cylinder ( \\( V_1 \\) )**:\n   - The cylinder circumscribed around the sphere has a height and diameter equal to the diameter of the sphere, which is \\( 2r \\).\n   - The radius of the cylinder is \\( r \\) and its height is \\( 2r \\).\n   - Volume \\( V_1 \\) is calculated as:\n     \\[\n     V_1 = \\pi r^2 \\cdot 2r = 2\\pi r^3\n     \\]\n\n2. **Volume of the Cone ( \\( V_2 \\) )**:\n   - For a cone circumscribed around the sphere, we need to relate the height \\( h \\) and base radius \\( R \\) of the cone such that the sphere is inscribed.\n   - Using geometric relationships and calculus, we derive the height \\( h \\) and base radius \\( R \\) of the cone. The critical point for minimizing the volume ratio is found when \\( h = 4r \\) and \\( R = r\\sqrt{2} \\).\n   - Substituting these values into the volume formula for the cone:\n     \\[\n     V_2 = \\frac{1}{3} \\pi (r\\sqrt{2})^2 \\cdot 4r = \\frac{1}{3} \\pi \\cdot 2r^2 \\cdot 4r = \\frac{8}{3} \\pi r^3\n     \\]\n\n3. **Ratio \\( \\dfrac{V_2}{V_1} \\)**:\n   - Substituting the volumes \\( V_1 \\) and \\( V_2 \\) into the ratio:\n     \\[\n     \\dfrac{V_2}{V_1} = \\dfrac{\\frac{8}{3} \\pi r^3}{2 \\pi r^3} = \\dfrac{8}{3} \\div 2 = \\dfrac{4}{3}\n     \\]\n\nThus, the minimum value of the ratio \\( \\dfrac{V_2}{V_1} \\) is \\(\\boxed{\\dfrac{4}{3}}\\).", "ground_truth_solution": "\nGiven a sphere of radius $r$, we are asked to find the minimum value of the ratio $\\frac{V_{2}}{V_{1}}$, where $V_1$ is the volume of the cylinder circumscribed around the sphere, and $V_2$ is the volume of the cone circumscribed around the sphere. \n\n1. **Calculate the volume of the circumscribed cylinder ($V_1$):**\n   \n   Since the cylinder is circumscribed around the sphere, the height of the cylinder is $2r$ (diameter of the sphere) and the radius of the cylinder base is also $r$.\n\n   \\[\n   V_1 = \\pi r^2 h = \\pi r^2 \\cdot 2r = 2\\pi r^3\n   \\]\n\n2. **Calculate the volume of the circumscribed cone ($V_2$):**\n\n   Let the radius of the base of the cone be $R$ and the height of the cone be $m$.\n\n   \\[\n   V_{2} = \\frac{1}{3} \\pi R^{2} m\n   \\]\n\n3. **Express the ratio $\\frac{V_{2}}{V_{1}}$:**\n\n   \\[\n   \\frac{V_{2}}{V_{1}} = \\frac{\\frac{1}{3} \\pi R^{2} m}{2 \\pi r^{3}} = \\frac{R^{2} m}{6 r^{3}}\n   \\]\n\n4. **Minimize $\\frac{R^{2} m}{6 r^{3}}$ by finding the minimum value of $R^2 m$:**\n\n   From the geometry of a cone circumscribed around a sphere, consider a vertical cross-section through the cone that includes the axis of symmetry. This will form two similar triangles: one triangle formed by the center of the sphere and the other triangle formed by the base circle of the cone.\n\n   \\[\n   \\frac{R}{m} = \\frac{r}{\\sqrt{(m-r)^2-r^2}}\n   \\]\n\n   Squaring both sides and rearranging terms:\n\n   \\[\n   \\frac{R^2}{m^2} = \\frac{r^2}{(m-r)^2-r^2} \n   \\]\n\n   Simplifying:\n\n   \\[\n   \\frac{R^2}{m^2} = \\frac{r^2}{m^2 - 2mr + r^2 - r^2} = \\frac{r^2}{m(m-2r)}\n   \\]\n\n   So,\n\n   \\[\n   R^2 m = \\frac{r^2 m^2}{m-2r}\n   \\]\n\n5. **Find the critical points to minimize $\\frac{r^2 m^2}{m-2r}$:**\n\n   Let \\( f(m) = \\frac{r^2 m^2}{m-2r} \\). \n\n   \\[\n   f'(m) = \\frac{d}{dm} \\left( \\frac{r^2 m^2}{m-2r} \\right)\n   \\]\n\n   Using quotient rule:\n\n   \\[\n   f'(m) = r^2 \\left( \\frac{(m-2r) \\cdot 2m \\cdot r^2 - m^2 \\cdot r^2}{(m-2r)^2} \\right) = \\frac{2r^2 m(m-2r) - r^2 m^2}{(m-2r)^2}\n   \\]\n\n   Simplifying the numerator to find where $f'(m) = 0$:\n\n   \\[\n   2r^2 m^2 - 4r^3 m - r^2 m^2 = r^2 m(m - 4r) = 0\n   \\]\n\n   Since $r^2 m \\neq 0$, we have:\n\n   \\[\n   m-4r = 0 \\implies m = 4r\n   \\]\n\n6. **Evaluate $R^2 m$ at $m = 4r$:**\n\n   Substituting $m = 4r$:\n\n   \\[\n   R^2 m = \\frac{r^2 (4r)^2}{4r - 2r} = \\frac{16 r^4}{2r} = 8r^3\n   \\]\n\n7. **Substitute back into the ratio:**\n\n   \\[\n   \\frac{V_{2}}{V_{1}} = \\frac{R^{2} m}{6 r^{3}} = \\frac{8 r^3}{6 r^3} = \\frac{8}{6} = \\frac{4}{3}\n   \\]\n\nThus, the minimum value of the ratio $\\frac{V_2}{V_1}$ is\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\frac{4}{3}}\n\\]", "domain": "math", "source": "numina_math", "test_cases": null, "starter_code": null}, {"problem": "Calculate the greatest common divisor of 15 and 70.", "deepseek_reasoning": "Okay, so I need to find the greatest common divisor (GCD) of 15 and 70. Hmm, let me think about how to approach this. I remember that the GCD is the largest number that divides both numbers without leaving a remainder. So I need to find all the divisors of 15 and 70 and then see which is the biggest one they have in common.\n\nFirst, let me list the divisors of 15. The factors of 15 are numbers that multiply together to give 15. Starting from 1: 1 \u00d7 15 = 15. Then 3 \u00d7 5 = 15. So the divisors of 15 are 1, 3, 5, and 15.\n\nNow for 70. Let's do the same. 1 \u00d7 70 = 70. Then 2 \u00d7 35 = 70. Next, 5 \u00d7 14 = 70. Also, 7 \u00d7 10 = 70. So the divisors of 70 are 1, 2, 5, 7, 10, 14, 35, and 70.\n\nNow I need to find the common divisors in both lists. Looking at the divisors of 15: 1, 3, 5, 15. And for 70: 1, 2, 5, 7, 10, 14, 35, 70. The common ones here are 1 and 5. Out of these, the greatest one is 5. So the GCD should be 5.\n\nWait, let me double-check to make sure I didn't miss any numbers. Maybe I can use another method, like the Euclidean algorithm, to confirm. The Euclidean algorithm is a more efficient way, especially for larger numbers. Let me try that.\n\nThe Euclidean algorithm involves dividing the larger number by the smaller one and then replacing the larger number with the smaller one and the smaller number with the remainder, repeating until the remainder is zero. The last non-zero remainder is the GCD.\n\nSo here, 70 divided by 15. 15 times 4 is 60, which leaves a remainder of 10 (70 - 60 = 10). Now, replace the numbers with 15 and 10. \n\nNext step: 15 divided by 10. 10 times 1 is 10, remainder 5 (15 - 10 = 5). Now replace with 10 and 5.\n\nNext: 10 divided by 5. 5 times 2 is 10, remainder 0. Since the remainder is 0, the last non-zero remainder is 5. So that confirms the GCD is indeed 5.\n\nJust to be thorough, another way is prime factorization. Let's break down both numbers into their prime factors.\n\n15 can be factored into 3 \u00d7 5.\n\n70 can be factored into 2 \u00d7 5 \u00d7 7.\n\nThe common prime factor here is 5. So the GCD is 5, raised to the lowest power present in both factorizations, which is 5^1 = 5. Yep, that matches the previous results.\n\nI think that's solid. All methods point to 5 being the GCD of 15 and 70. I can't think of any other numbers that divide both without a remainder. Let's see, does 15 divide 70? 70 divided by 15 is about 4.666..., so no. What about 5? 70 divided by 5 is 14, and 15 divided by 5 is 3. Both are integers, so 5 works. And there's nothing higher than 5 that works. So yeah, 5 is definitely the right answer.", "deepseek_solution": "The greatest common divisor (GCD) of 15 and 70 can be determined using multiple methods:\n\n1. **Listing Divisors**:\n   - Divisors of 15: 1, 3, 5, 15.\n   - Divisors of 70: 1, 2, 5, 7, 10, 14, 35, 70.\n   - Common divisors: 1, 5.  \n     **GCD = 5**.\n\n2. **Euclidean Algorithm**:\n   - \\( 70 \\div 15 = 4 \\) with remainder \\( 10 \\).\n   - \\( 15 \\div 10 = 1 \\) with remainder \\( 5 \\).\n   - \\( 10 \\div 5 = 2 \\) with remainder \\( 0 \\).  \n     **GCD = 5** (last non-zero remainder).\n\n3. **Prime Factorization**:\n   - \\( 15 = 3 \\times 5 \\).\n   - \\( 70 = 2 \\times 5 \\times 7 \\).  \n     Common prime: \\( 5 \\).  \n     **GCD = 5**.\n\n**Final Answer**: The greatest common divisor of 15 and 70 is \\(\\boxed{5}\\).", "ground_truth_solution": ": \nPour trouver le plus grand diviseur commun (PGCD) de 15 et 70, nous allons utiliser l'algorithme d'Euclide.\n\n1. **Division de 70 par 15 :**\n   - Trouver le quotient et le reste lorsque 70 est divis\u00e9 par 15.\n   - \\( 70 = 15 \\times 4 + 10 \\)\n   - Le reste est donc 10.\n\n2. **Division de 15 par 10 :**\n   - Trouver le quotient et le reste lorsque 15 est divis\u00e9 par 10.\n   - \\( 15 = 10 \\times 1 + 5 \\)\n   - Le reste est donc 5.\n\n3. **Division de 10 par 5 :**\n   - Trouver le quotient et le reste lorsque 10 est divis\u00e9 par 5.\n   - \\( 10 = 5 \\times 2 + 0 \\)\n   - Le reste est donc 0.\n\n4. **Conclusion :**\n   - Lorsque le reste devient 0, le diviseur courant est le PGCD. \n   - Donc, \\( 70 \\wedge 15 = 5 \\).\n\n\\(\\boxed{5}\\)", "domain": "math", "source": "numina_math", "test_cases": null, "starter_code": null}, {"problem": "Let real numbers \\( x_{1}, x_{2}, \\ldots, x_{n} \\) satisfy \\( 0 < x_{1} < x_{2} < \\cdots < x_{n} < 1 \\) and set \\( x_{0}=0, x_{n+1}=1 \\). Suppose that these numbers satisfy the following system of equations:\n\\[ \n\\sum_{j=0, j \\neq i}^{n+1} \\frac{1}{x_{i}-x_{j}}=0 \\quad \\text{where } i=1,2, \\ldots, n.\n\\]\n\nProve that \\( x_{n+1-i}=1-x_{i} \\) for \\( i=1,2, \\ldots, n \\).", "deepseek_reasoning": "Okay, so I have this problem here where there are real numbers \\( x_1, x_2, \\ldots, x_n \\) arranged in increasing order between 0 and 1. They've also set \\( x_0 = 0 \\) and \\( x_{n+1} = 1 \\). The key thing is that these numbers satisfy a system of equations given by the sum over \\( j \\) (excluding \\( j = i \\)) of \\( \\frac{1}{x_i - x_j} = 0 \\) for each \\( i \\) from 1 to \\( n \\). The task is to prove that \\( x_{n+1-i} = 1 - x_i \\) for each \\( i \\), meaning the points are symmetric around 0.5. \n\nAlright, let me start by understanding what this system of equations represents. Each \\( x_i \\) (from 1 to \\( n \\)) has a condition where the sum of the reciprocals of its differences with all other points (including \\( x_0 = 0 \\) and \\( x_{n+1} = 1 \\)) is zero. That feels similar to some equilibrium condition where the forces or potentials balance out. Maybe like charges arranged on a line where the electric field at each charge due to the others is zero? That might be a useful analogy.\n\nSo, if I think of each \\( x_i \\) as a charge, the electric field at each charge would be the sum of the forces from all other charges. If that sum is zero, the charge is in equilibrium. Interesting. In such cases, symmetric configurations often satisfy the equilibrium conditions. So maybe the symmetry around 0.5 is necessary here.\n\nLet me try plugging in the symmetry condition into the equations to see if it holds. Suppose \\( x_{n+1 - i} = 1 - x_i \\). Then, for each \\( i \\), we can pair each term in the sum with its symmetric counterpart. Let's take a specific \\( i \\), say \\( x_k \\), and its symmetric counterpart \\( x_{n+1 - k} = 1 - x_k \\). Then, for each \\( x_j \\), there's a corresponding \\( x_{n+1 - j} = 1 - x_j \\). \n\nSo, considering the equation for \\( x_k \\), the sum over \\( j \\neq k \\) of \\( 1/(x_k - x_j) = 0 \\). If we substitute each \\( x_j \\) with \\( 1 - x_{n+1 - j} \\), maybe the terms will cancel out when considering their symmetric counterparts. Let's check.\n\nTake the term \\( 1/(x_k - x_j) \\). Its symmetric counterpart would be \\( 1/(x_{n+1 - k} - x_{n+1 - j}) \\). Substituting the symmetric expressions, this becomes \\( 1/((1 - x_k) - (1 - x_j)) = 1/(x_j - x_k) = -1/(x_k - x_j) \\). So each term and its symmetric counterpart add up to zero. Therefore, if the sum for \\( x_k \\) is zero, then the sum for \\( x_{n+1 - k} \\) would also be zero, but with all terms negated. Wait, but the sum for \\( x_{n+1 - k} \\) would be the sum over \\( j \\neq n+1 -k \\) of \\( 1/(x_{n+1 -k} - x_j) \\). If we substitute \\( j \\) with \\( n+1 - m \\), then each term becomes \\( 1/(x_{n+1 -k} - x_{n+1 -m}) = 1/((1 - x_k) - (1 - x_m)) = 1/(x_m - x_k) = -1/(x_k - x_m) \\). \n\nBut since the original sum for \\( x_k \\) is zero, the sum over \\( m \\neq k \\) of \\( -1/(x_k - x_m) \\) would also be zero. Hence, the sum for \\( x_{n+1 -k} \\) is the negative of the sum for \\( x_k \\), which is zero. Therefore, if the original equations hold for \\( x_k \\), they would also hold for \\( x_{n+1 -k} \\). That suggests that the symmetric configuration is consistent with the equations. However, this only shows that if the configuration is symmetric, then the equations hold for all points. But we need to prove that the configuration must be symmetric given that the equations hold. So uniqueness is key here. \n\nAlternatively, maybe we can consider that the system of equations has a unique solution, which must therefore be symmetric. But how do we know the solution is unique? The problem states that the points satisfy the given system, and we need to prove symmetry. So perhaps the system forces the symmetry.\n\nAnother approach could be to consider a function related to the given equations. Let me think. Suppose we define a function \\( f(z) = \\prod_{j=0}^{n+1} (z - x_j) \\). Then, the derivative of \\( f(z) \\) at \\( z = x_i \\) is \\( f'(x_i) = \\prod_{j \\neq i} (x_i - x_j) \\). The given equations involve sums of \\( 1/(x_i - x_j) \\). Maybe there's a connection here with logarithmic derivatives or something similar. \n\nRecall that the logarithmic derivative of \\( f(z) \\) is \\( f'(z)/f(z) = \\sum_{j=0}^{n+1} 1/(z - x_j) \\). But in our problem, the equations are \\( \\sum_{j \\neq i} 1/(x_i - x_j) = 0 \\). Wait, that's almost the logarithmic derivative evaluated at \\( z = x_i \\), except the sum includes all \\( j \\neq i \\), but the logarithmic derivative would be \\( \\sum_{j=0}^{n+1} 1/(x_i - x_j) \\). So our equations are exactly the logarithmic derivative at \\( x_i \\), minus the term \\( 1/(x_i - x_i) \\), which is undefined, but since we exclude \\( j = i \\), the equations are \\( f'(x_i)/f(x_i) - 1/(x_i - x_i) = 0 \\). Wait, that's not helpful because of the undefined term. Maybe another angle.\n\nAlternatively, if we consider the equations \\( \\sum_{j \\neq i} 1/(x_i - x_j) = 0 \\), which is equivalent to \\( \\sum_{j=0}^{n+1} \\frac{1}{x_i - x_j} = 0 \\) because when \\( j = i \\), the term is excluded. So this is equivalent to the logarithmic derivative \\( f'(x_i)/f(x_i) = 0 \\), but \\( f(x_i) = 0 \\), so actually, \\( f'(x_i) \\) would be involved. Wait, no, because \\( f(z) \\) has a root at each \\( x_j \\), so \\( f'(x_i) = \\prod_{j \\neq i} (x_i - x_j) \\). Therefore, the sum \\( \\sum_{j \\neq i} 1/(x_i - x_j) = \\frac{f''(x_i)}{2f'(x_i)} \\)? Hmm, not sure. Maybe not the right path.\n\nWait, let's think again. The sum \\( \\sum_{j \\neq i} \\frac{1}{x_i - x_j} \\) is equal to \\( \\frac{f''(x_i)}{2f'(x_i)} \\), but I might be misremembering the formula. Alternatively, perhaps it's related to the derivative of the logarithmic derivative. Let me check.\n\nIf \\( f(z) = \\prod_{j=0}^{n+1} (z - x_j) \\), then \\( \\ln f(z) = \\sum_{j=0}^{n+1} \\ln(z - x_j) \\). Then, the first derivative is \\( f'(z)/f(z) = \\sum_{j=0}^{n+1} 1/(z - x_j) \\). The second derivative would be \\( (f''(z)f(z) - (f'(z))^2)/f(z)^2 = \\sum_{j=0}^{n+1} -1/(z - x_j)^2 \\). But not sure how this helps. \n\nAlternatively, since the equations state that for each \\( x_i \\), the sum of reciprocals of \\( x_i - x_j \\) is zero, maybe this implies that the polynomial \\( f(z) \\) satisfies certain properties. If all these sums are zero, perhaps \\( f(z) \\) is related to a certain type of polynomial. \n\nWait, let's think about the case when the roots are symmetric. If \\( x_{n+1 - i} = 1 - x_i \\), then the polynomial \\( f(z) = \\prod_{j=0}^{n+1} (z - x_j) \\) would satisfy \\( f(z) = \\pm f(1 - z) \\). Let's check. If we replace \\( z \\) with \\( 1 - z \\), then \\( f(1 - z) = \\prod_{j=0}^{n+1} (1 - z - x_j) = \\prod_{j=0}^{n+1} (1 - x_j - z) \\). If the roots are symmetric, then \\( 1 - x_j = x_{n+1 - j} \\), so \\( f(1 - z) = \\prod_{j=0}^{n+1} (x_{n+1 - j} - z) = \\prod_{k=0}^{n+1} (x_k - z) = (-1)^{n+2} f(z) \\). Therefore, \\( f(1 - z) = (-1)^{n+2} f(z) \\). So depending on whether \\( n+2 \\) is even or odd, this would give \\( f(1 - z) = \\pm f(z) \\). That's interesting. If the polynomial is symmetric in this way, then it satisfies this functional equation. \n\nBut how does this relate to the given equations? If the polynomial is symmetric, then maybe the conditions of the problem are satisfied. Alternatively, if the conditions of the problem are satisfied, then the polynomial must be symmetric. So perhaps we need to show that if all the sums \\( \\sum_{j \\neq i} 1/(x_i - x_j) = 0 \\), then \\( f(z) \\) must satisfy \\( f(z) = \\pm f(1 - z) \\), leading to the symmetry of roots. \n\nAlternatively, suppose we construct a polynomial \\( f(z) \\) with roots at \\( x_j \\). Then, consider another polynomial \\( g(z) = f(1 - z) \\). If \\( f(z) \\) is symmetric, then \\( g(z) = \\pm f(z) \\). Let's compute the derivative of \\( f(z) \\). If \\( f(z) = \\prod_{j=0}^{n+1} (z - x_j) \\), then \\( f'(z) = \\sum_{k=0}^{n+1} \\prod_{j \\neq k} (z - x_j) \\). Then, at each root \\( x_i \\), \\( f'(x_i) = \\prod_{j \\neq i} (x_i - x_j) \\). \n\nGiven the equations \\( \\sum_{j \\neq i} \\frac{1}{x_i - x_j} = 0 \\), this sum is equal to \\( \\frac{d}{dz} \\left( \\ln f(z) \\right) \\bigg|_{z = x_i} \\) minus the term \\( \\frac{1}{x_i - x_i} \\), which isn't there. Wait, actually, the logarithmic derivative is \\( \\frac{f'(z)}{f(z)} = \\sum_{j=0}^{n+1} \\frac{1}{z - x_j} \\). But when \\( z = x_i \\), \\( f(z) = 0 \\), so the logarithmic derivative is undefined. However, the sum in the problem is exactly the sum over \\( j \\neq i \\) of \\( 1/(x_i - x_j) \\), which is equal to \\( \\lim_{z \\to x_i} \\left( \\frac{f'(z)}{f(z)} - \\frac{1}{z - x_i} \\right) \\). Since \\( f(z) \\) has a simple zero at \\( x_i \\), near \\( z = x_i \\), \\( f(z) \\approx (z - x_i)f'(x_i) \\), so \\( \\frac{f'(z)}{f(z)} \\approx \\frac{1}{z - x_i} + \\frac{f''(x_i)}{2f'(x_i)} \\). Therefore, the limit as \\( z \\to x_i \\) of \\( \\frac{f'(z)}{f(z)} - \\frac{1}{z - x_i} \\) is \\( \\frac{f''(x_i)}{2f'(x_i)} \\). So according to this, the given equations imply that \\( \\frac{f''(x_i)}{2f'(x_i)} = 0 \\), i.e., \\( f''(x_i) = 0 \\) for each \\( i \\). \n\nWait, this is interesting. If \\( \\sum_{j \\neq i} \\frac{1}{x_i - x_j} = 0 \\), then \\( f''(x_i) = 0 \\). So each \\( x_i \\) is not only a root of \\( f(z) \\) but also a root of \\( f''(z) \\). Therefore, \\( f(z) \\) and \\( f''(z) \\) share common roots at each \\( x_i \\). This suggests that \\( f''(z) \\) is divisible by \\( f(z) \\), but since \\( f(z) \\) is a polynomial of degree \\( n+2 \\), and \\( f''(z) \\) is of degree \\( n \\), unless \\( n \\geq n+2 \\), which is not possible. Therefore, the only way this can happen is if \\( f''(z) \\) is identically zero, but that can't be unless \\( f(z) \\) is linear, which would require \\( n+2 = 1 \\), i.e., \\( n = -1 \\), which is impossible. So something's wrong here.\n\nWait, maybe my reasoning is off. Let me double-check. If \\( f(z) = \\prod_{j=0}^{n+1} (z - x_j) \\), then \\( f''(z) \\) is a polynomial of degree \\( n \\). For each root \\( x_i \\) of \\( f(z) \\), \\( f''(x_i) = 0 \\). So we have \\( n \\) roots (since \\( i = 1, \\ldots, n \\)) but \\( f''(z) \\) is degree \\( n \\), so it can have at most \\( n \\) roots (counting multiplicities). However, the \\( x_i \\) for \\( i = 1, \\ldots, n \\) are distinct, so \\( f''(z) \\) must be a constant multiple of \\( \\prod_{i=1}^n (z - x_i) \\). But \\( f''(z) \\) is a degree \\( n \\) polynomial. Wait, but \\( f(z) \\) is degree \\( n+2 \\), so \\( f''(z) \\) is degree \\( n \\). So if all the roots \\( x_i \\) (from 1 to n) are roots of \\( f''(z) \\), then \\( f''(z) = C \\prod_{i=1}^n (z - x_i) \\). But since \\( f(z) \\) also has roots at \\( x_0 = 0 \\) and \\( x_{n+1} = 1 \\), how does this relate?\n\nAlternatively, maybe consider that \\( f(z) \\) has roots at 0, 1, and the \\( x_i \\). Then, \\( f''(z) \\) has roots at each \\( x_i \\). So, perhaps there's a relation between \\( f(z) \\) and \\( f''(z) \\). Let's write \\( f(z) = z(z - 1) \\prod_{i=1}^n (z - x_i) \\). Then, \\( f''(z) \\) would be the second derivative of this product. Not sure if that helps. Maybe we can think of \\( f(z) \\) as a polynomial that satisfies a differential equation. For example, if \\( f''(z) = C \\prod_{i=1}^n (z - x_i) \\), but \\( f(z) = z(z - 1) \\prod_{i=1}^n (z - x_i) \\). Therefore, \\( f(z) = z(z - 1) \\cdot \\frac{f''(z)}{C} \\). So \\( f(z) \\) is proportional to \\( z(z - 1) f''(z) \\). That gives a differential equation: \\( f(z) = k z(z - 1) f''(z) \\), where \\( k \\) is a constant. Maybe solving this differential equation would give us the form of \\( f(z) \\), leading to symmetry.\n\nBut differential equations can be tricky. Let's see if there's a known polynomial that satisfies such a symmetry. For example, if \\( f(z) = (-1)^{n+2} f(1 - z) \\), then the roots are symmetric around 0.5. If that's the case, then the differential equation should respect that symmetry. Suppose \\( f(z) = \\pm f(1 - z) \\). Then, taking derivatives, \\( f'(z) = \\mp f'(1 - z) \\) and \\( f''(z) = \\pm f''(1 - z) \\). If we plug into the differential equation \\( f(z) = k z(z - 1) f''(z) \\), then substituting \\( z \\) with \\( 1 - z \\), we get \\( f(1 - z) = k (1 - z)(-z) f''(1 - z) \\). If \\( f(z) = \\pm f(1 - z) \\), then \\( \\pm f(z) = k (1 - z)(-z) (\\pm f''(z)) \\), which simplifies to \\( f(z) = -k z(1 - z) f''(z) \\). Comparing with the original equation \\( f(z) = k z(z - 1) f''(z) \\), we see that \\( -k z(1 - z) f''(z) = k z(z - 1) f''(z) \\), which holds true because \\( z(z - 1) = -z(1 - z) \\). So the differential equation is compatible with the symmetry. Therefore, if \\( f(z) \\) satisfies the differential equation and the symmetry condition, then the roots are symmetric. \n\nBut how do we know that the only solution to the differential equation \\( f(z) = k z(z - 1) f''(z) \\) with the given boundary conditions (roots at 0,1 and the \\( x_i \\)) is the symmetric one? That requires more analysis.\n\nAlternatively, consider specific cases. Let's take n=1. Then there is one variable x1, and the equations are:\n\nFor i=1: 1/(x1 - 0) + 1/(x1 - 1) = 0.\n\nSo 1/x1 + 1/(x1 - 1) = 0.\n\nMultiplying both sides by x1(x1 - 1):\n\n(x1 - 1) + x1 = 0 => 2x1 - 1 = 0 => x1 = 1/2. So in this case, symmetry holds.\n\nFor n=2, variables x1 and x2. The equations are:\n\nFor i=1: 1/(x1 - 0) + 1/(x1 - x2) + 1/(x1 - 1) = 0.\n\nFor i=2: 1/(x2 - 0) + 1/(x2 - x1) + 1/(x2 - 1) = 0.\n\nAssuming symmetry, x2 = 1 - x1. Let's check if this satisfies the equations.\n\nLet\u2019s set x2 = 1 - x1. Then for i=1:\n\n1/x1 + 1/(x1 - (1 - x1)) + 1/(x1 - 1) = 1/x1 + 1/(2x1 - 1) + 1/(x1 - 1).\n\nSimilarly, for i=2:\n\n1/(1 - x1) + 1/((1 - x1) - x1) + 1/((1 - x1) - 1) = 1/(1 - x1) + 1/(1 - 2x1) + 1/(-x1).\n\nBut note that equation for i=2 is the same as equation for i=1 with x1 replaced by 1 - x1. Therefore, if equation for i=1 holds, equation for i=2 also holds. Therefore, we can focus on solving for x1:\n\n1/x1 + 1/(2x1 - 1) + 1/(x1 - 1) = 0.\n\nLet me compute this:\n\nLet\u2019s denote x = x1.\n\nThen 1/x + 1/(2x - 1) + 1/(x - 1) = 0.\n\nCombine terms:\n\nFirst, combine 1/x and 1/(x - 1):\n\n1/x + 1/(x - 1) = (x - 1 + x)/(x(x - 1)) = (2x - 1)/(x(x - 1)).\n\nThen add 1/(2x - 1):\n\n(2x - 1)/(x(x - 1)) + 1/(2x - 1) = 0.\n\nMultiply both sides by x(x - 1)(2x - 1):\n\n(2x - 1)^2 + x(x - 1) = 0.\n\nExpand:\n\n(4x\u00b2 -4x +1) + x\u00b2 -x = 5x\u00b2 -5x +1 =0.\n\nSolve quadratic equation:\n\nx = [5 \u00b1 sqrt(25 - 20)] / 10 = [5 \u00b1 sqrt(5)] /10.\n\nThus, x1 =