hus, each \\( x_i \\) is a root of both \\( f(z) \\) and \\( f''(z) \\).\n\n3. **Symmetry Transformation:**\n   Consider the transformation \\( y_i = 1 - x_{n+1-i} \\). Applying this to the polynomial \\( f(z) \\) yields \\( f(1 - z) \\), which must also satisfy the same system of equations due to the problem's symmetry. This implies \\( f(1 - z) = \\pm f(z) \\).\n\n4. **Invariance Under Transformation:**\n   The system's equations are invariant under the transformation \\( x_i \\to 1 - x_{n+1-i} \\). Therefore, if \\( \\{x_i\\} \\) is a solution, \\( \\{y_i\\} = \\{1 - x_{n+1-i}\\} \\) is also a solution. Given the strict ordering \\( 0 < x_1 < \\cdots < x_n < 1 \\), the only configuration invariant under this transformation is the symmetric one where \\( x_{n+1-i} = 1 - x_i \\).\n\n5. **Uniqueness and Conclusion:**\n   The uniqueness of the solution (implicit from the equilibrium conditions and polynomial root properties) ensures no asymmetric solutions exist. Hence, the symmetry \\( x_{n+1-i} = 1 - x_i \\) must hold for all \\( i \\).\n\n**Final Answer:**\n\nBy leveraging the symmetry of the polynomial's roots and the invariance of the equilibrium conditions under reflection, we conclude that the points must satisfy \\( \\boxed{x_{n+1-i} = 1 - x_i} \\) for each \\( i = 1, 2, \\ldots, n \\).", "ground_truth_solution": "\nGiven the problem statement, we begin by considering the polynomial \\( P(x) = (x - x_0)(x - x_1) \\cdots (x - x_n)(x - x_{n+1}) \\). We know the following:\n\n- \\( x_0 = 0 \\)\n- \\( x_{n+1} = 1 \\)\n- \\( 0 < x_1 < x_2 < \\cdots < x_n < 1 \\)\n- The numbers \\( x_1, x_2, \\ldots, x_n \\) satisfy the given system of equations:\n  \\[\n  \\sum_{j=0, j \\neq i}^{n+1} \\frac{1}{x_i - x_j} = 0 \\quad \\text{for} \\quad i=1,2, \\ldots, n.\n  \\]\n\nNow, let's derive \\( P'(x) \\) and \\( P''(x) \\).\n\n1. **Calculating the first derivative \\( P'(x) \\)**:\n\n   \\[\n   P'(x) = \\sum_{j=0}^{n+1} \\frac{P(x)}{x - x_j}\n   \\]\n   Note that this follow