tion over some line l. For any vector v, R(v) is the reflection of v over l. Therefore, v + R(v) is twice the projection of v onto l. Therefore, AB + R(AB) is 2 Proj_l(AB), and similarly AC + R(AC) is 2 Proj_l(AC). Therefore, both B' - A' and C' - A' are vectors along l scaled by 2/(k + 1). Therefore, these vectors are scalar multiples of each other if Proj_l(AB) and Proj_l(AC) are scalar multiples. However, unless AB and AC are such that their projections onto l are scalar multiples, which is not necessarily true.\n\nWait, this seems contradictory. If l is the reflection line, then the projection of AB onto l is a vector along l, and similarly for AC. But AB and AC are arbitrary vectors unless the triangle ABC is degenerate. So unless l is chosen in some special way relative to ABC, their projections might not be colinear.\n\nWait, but in our case, the reflection R is part of the similarity transformation that maps ABC to A1B1C1. So the line l over which we reflect is the axis of the similarity transformation. Since ABC and A1B1C1 are similar with opposite orientation, the transformation can be expressed as a reflection over l followed by a homothety (scaling) with ratio 1/k.\n\nTherefore, the line l is the axis of the reflection in the similarity transformation. Therefore, this line l is fixed by the transformation. Hence, points on l are mapped to themselves after reflection and scaling. Therefore, the projections of AB, AC onto l are related to the transformation.\n\nBut perhaps the key insight is that vectors AB + R(AB) and AC + R(AC) are both in the direction of l, hence colinear. Therefore, vectors B' - A' and C' - A' are both scalar multiples along l, hence colinear. Therefore, points A', B', C' must lie on a line parallel to l.\n\nWait, but the direction of l is arbitrary. However, since l is the reflection axis in the similarity transformation, and the points A', B', C' are combinations of original points and their reflections over l, scaled appropriately.