 maps \\(\\triangle ABC\\) to \\(\\triangle ADC\\). Since \\(O_1\\) and \\(O_2\\) are the circumcenters of \\(\\triangle ABC\\) and \\(\\triangle ADC\\) respectively, this spiral similarity maps \\(O_1\\) to \\(O_2\\). Consequently, the segments \\(PO_1\\) and \\(PO_2\\) are related by the scaling factor of the spiral similarity, and the angle \\(\\angle O_1PO_2\\) is equal to the rotation angle of the spiral similarity.\n\n3. **Proportional Sides and Equal Angles**: The spiral similarity ensures that the sides \\(O_1B\\) and \\(O_2D\\) (which are radii of the circumcircles of \\(\\triangle ABC\\) and \\(\\triangle ADC\\)) are proportional. Additionally, the angles \\(\\angle PO_1B\\) and \\(\\angle PO_2D\\) are equal due to the rotation component of the spiral similarity.\n\n4. **SAS Similarity**: By the properties of spiral similarity, we have:\n   - \\(\\angle PO_1B = \\angle PO_2D\\)\n   - \\(\\frac{PO_1}{PO_2} = \\frac{O_1B}{O_2D}\\)\n\n   These conditions satisfy the SAS (Side-Angle-Side) similarity criterion, proving that \\(\\triangle PO_1B \\sim \\triangle PO_2D\\).\n\nThus, the similarity of \\(\\triangle P O_1 B\\) and \\(\\triangle P O_2 D\\) is established, leading to the final answer:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\triangle P O_1 B \\sim \\triangle P O_2 D}\n\\]", "ground_truth_solution": "\n1. Construct the following setup as described in the problem.\n   \n   - Let $P$ be a point such that $\\angle PAB = \\angle CAD$ and $\\angle PCB = \\angle ACD$.\n   - Let $O_1$ be the circumcenter of $\\triangle ABC$, and $O_2$ be the circumcenter of $\\triangle ADC$.\n\n2. Extend $CP$ to intersect the circumcircle of $\\triangle ABC$ at $Q$.\n\n3. Draw segments $QA$, $QB$, $QO_1$, and $AO_2$.\n\n4. To prove $\\triangle P O_{1} B \\sim \\triangle P O_{2} D$, we first show:\n   $$\\triangle O_{1} B Q \\sim \\triangle O_{2} A D.$$\n\n5. Note the following:\n   \n   - $\\angle BO_1Q = 2 \\angle BCQ = 2 \\angle ACD = \\angle AO_2D$.\n   - Hence, $\\triangle O_1 B Q \\sim \\triangle O_2 A D$ by AA similarity (Angle-Angle Similarity Criterion).\n\n6. Next, within $\\triangle PAQ$, using the Law of Sines, we get:\n   $$\n   \\frac{PQ}{PA} = \\frac{\\sin \\angle PAQ}{\\sin \\angle PQA}\n   = \\frac{\\sin (\\angle PAB + \\angle BAQ)}{\\sin \\angle CBA}\n   = \\frac{\\sin (\\angle DAC + \\angle BCQ)}{\\sin \\angle CBA}\n   = \\frac{\\sin (\\angle DAC + \\angle DCA)}{\\sin \\angle CBA}\n   = \\frac{\\sin \\left(180^\\circ - \\angle CDA\\right)}{\\sin \\angle CBA}\n   = \\frac{\\sin \\angle CDA}{\\sin \\angle CBA}\n   = \\frac{\\frac{AC}{R_2}}{\\frac{AC}{R_1}}\n   = \\frac{R_1}{R_2},\n   $$\n   \n   where $R_1$ and $R_2$ are the circumradii of $\\triangle ABC$ and $\\triangle ADC$, respectively.\n\n7. Therefore, we have:\n   $$\n   \\frac{PQ}{PA} = \\frac{R_1}{R_2}.\n   $$\n\n8. Since:\n   $$BQ = 2 R_1 \\sin \\angle BCQ = 2 R_1 \\sin \\angle ACD,$$\n   $$DA = 2 R_2 \\sin \\angle ACD,$$\n   we then find:\n   $$\n   \\frac{BQ}{DA} = \\frac{R_1}{R_2}.\n   $$\n\n9. Consequently:\n   $$\n   \\frac{PQ}{PA} = \\frac{BQ}{DA}.\n   $$\n\n10. Also, we have:\n    $$\n    \\angle BQP = \\angle BAC = \\angle PAD.\n    $$\n11. Thus, we conclude $\\triangle PQB \\sim \\triangle PAD$ by Side-Angle-Side similarity (SAS Similarity Criterion).\n\n12. Since $O_1$ and $O_2$ are the circumcenters of $\\triangle PQB$ and $\\triangle PAD$ respectively, they correspond to homologous points of these two similar triangles.\n\n13. Therefore, by combining the previously derived result $\\triangle O_{1} B Q \\sim \\triangle O_{2} A D$, $O_1$ and $O_2$ are corresponding points in $\\triangle PQB$ and $\\triangle PAD$. So, we finally establish that:\n    $$\n    \\triangle P O_1 B \\sim \\triangle P O_2 D.\n    $$\n\n### Conclusion:\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\triangle P O_1 B \\sim \\triangle P O_2 D}\n\\]", "domain": "math", "source": "numina_math", "test_cases": null, "starter_code": null}, {"problem": "The radii ratio of two concentric circles is \\( p \\). The diameter \\( AD \\) of the 