 \\alpha + 4t^2 \\sin^2 \\alpha = 16,\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   t^2 (\\cos^2 \\alpha + 4 \\sin^2 \\alpha) + t(4 \\cos \\alpha + 8 \\sin \\alpha) + 8 - 16 = 0,\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   t^2 (1 + 3 \\sin^2 \\alpha) + t (4 \\cos \\alpha + 8 \\sin \\alpha) - 8 = 0.\n   \\]\n\n8. This is a quadratic equation in \\( t \\):\n   \\[\n   (1 + 3 \\sin^2 \\alpha)t^2 + (4 \\cos \\alpha + 8 \\sin \\alpha)t - 8 = 0.\n   \\]\n\n9. By Vieta's formulas, the sum and product of the roots \\( t_1 \\) and \\( t_2 \\) are:\n   \\[\n   t_1 + t_2 = -\\frac{4 \\cos \\alpha + 8 \\sin \\alpha}{1 + 3 \\sin^2 \\alpha},\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   t_1 t_2 = -\\frac{8}{1 + 3 \\sin^2 \\alpha}.\n   \\]\n\n10. Given \\( M \\) is one-third of the way along \\( AB \\), we have either:\n    \\[\n    \\frac{t_1}{t_2} = -2 \\quad \\text{or} \\quad \\frac{t_2}{t_1} = -2.\n    \\]\n\n11. Thus:\n    \\[\n    \\frac{(t_1 + t_2)^2}{t_1 t_2} = -\\frac{(4 \\cos \\alpha + 8 \\sin \\alpha)^2}{8 (1 + 3 \\sin^2 \\alpha)} = -\\frac{1}{2}.\n    \\]\n\n12. Simplifying the above expression:\n    \\[\n    (4 \\cos \\alpha + 8 \\sin \\alpha)^2 = 8 (1 + 3 \\sin^2 \\alpha).\n    \\]\n\n13. Expanding and simplifying:\n    \\[\n    16 (\\cos \\alpha + 2 \\sin \\alpha)^2 = 8 (1 + 3 \\sin^2 \\alpha),\n    \\]\n\n14. Let:\n    \\[\n    x = \\tan \\alpha.\n    \\]\n    Substituting, we get:\n    \\[\n    (2 \\cos \\alpha + 4 \\sin \\alpha)^2 = \\cos^2 \\alpha + 4 \\sin^2 \\alpha,\n    \\]\n    Expands to:\n    \\[\n    4 \\cos^2 \\alpha + 16 \\sin^2 \\alpha + 16 \\sin \\alpha \\cos \\alpha = \\cos^2 \\alpha + 12 \\sin^2 \\alpha,\n    \\]\n    Simplified equation is:\n    \\[\n    3 \\cos^2 \\alpha + 12 \\sin^2 \\alpha + 16 \\sin \\alpha \\cos \\alpha = 0.\n    \\]\n\n15. Dividing through by \\(\\cos^2 \\alpha\\):\n    \\[\n    3 + \\frac{12 \\sin^2 \\alpha}{\\cos^2 \\alpha} + \\frac{16 \\sin \\alpha \\cos \\alpha}{\\cos^2 \\alpha} = 0,\n    \\]\n    \\[\n    3 + 12 \\tan^2 \\alpha + 16 \\tan \\alpha = 0.\n    \\]\n\n16. The quadratic equation in \\