ence, IM = IN, so I is the midpoint of MN. \n\nBut we need to show that I is the midpoint of AB. How to bridge this gap? \n\nWait, consider that line MN is the common external tangent to both circles. The midpoint of MN is I, but also, the line AB is the radical axis. There's a theorem that states that the midpoint of the common external tangent lies on the radical axis. But here, the radical axis is AB, and we've shown that I is the midpoint of MN and lies on AB. But the problem wants us to show that I is the midpoint of AB, not just lying on AB. \n\nThis suggests that there might be an additional property or a specific configuration where the midpoint of the common external tangent MN coincides with the midpoint of AB. However, in general, this isn't necessarily true unless certain conditions are met. \n\nWait, but the problem states that the circles intersect at A and B, so they are not concentric and have two intersection points. The common tangent MN is such that M is on C\u2081 and N is on C\u2082. The line MN is a common external tangent. \n\nPerhaps using the fact that the midpoint of AB is the only point on AB that has equal power with respect to both circles in a symmetric sense. But since all points on AB have equal power with respect to both circles, this doesn't differentiate the midpoint. \n\nAlternatively, using homothety again. The homothety that sends C\u2081 to C\u2082 maps M to N and A to B since A and B are common points. The center of this homothety must lie at the intersection of lines MANB. Since A and B are on both circles, the homothety center lies on AB. Also, since M and N are corresponding points under homothety, the center lies on line MN. Therefore, the homothety center is the intersection of AB and MN, which is point I. \n\nTherefore, point I is the center of the homothety that maps C\u2081 to C\u2082. This homothety maps A to B and M to N. Since homothety preserves ratios, the ratio of homothety k is the ratio of corresponding lengths. Therefore, k = BN/AM = BA/AB = -1 if it's a reflection. But since it's a homothety, the ratio is determined by the sizes of the circles. \n\nWait, homothety center I maps A to B and M to N. Therefore, the lines IA and IB are related by the homothety. The homothety center I maps A to B, so the ratio is IB/IA = k. Similarly, it maps M to N, so IN/IM = k. Therefore, k = IB/IA = IN/IM. \n\nBut from power of a point, we have IA * IB = IM * IN. Let\u2019s write IN = (IB/IA) * IM. Substitute into IA * IB = IM * IN:\n\nIA * IB = IM * (IB/IA * IM) \u21d2 IA * IB = (IM\u00b2 * IB)/IA \u21d2 IA\u00b2 * IB = IM\u00b2 * IB \u21d2 IA\u00b2 = IM\u00b2 \u21d2 IA = IM\n\nSimilarly, since k = IB/IA = IN/IM, and IA = IM, then k = IB/IM = IN/IM \u21d2 IB = IN\n\nTherefore, IA = IM and IB = IN\n\nBut from power of a point, IA * IB = IM * IN, which becomes IA * IB = IA * IB, which holds. \n\nTherefore, under homothety centered at I with ratio k = IB/IA, maps A to B and M to N. If IA = IM and IB = IN, then k = IB/IA = IN/IM = (IB)/(IA) \u21d2 consistent. \n\nBut how does this help us show that IA = IB? \n\nIf we can show that the homothety ratio k is -1, implying a reflection, then IA = IB. But unless the circles are congruent, k isn't necessarily -1. However, in our case, the homothety maps C\u2081 to C\u2082, which are two intersecting circles with a common tangent. The ratio k would be the ratio of the radii of C\u2082 to C\u2081. \n\nBut unless we have more information, we can't assume k = -1. Therefore, this approach might not lead us to the conclusion. \n\nWait, but we have that IA = IM and IB = IN from the homothety. Since I is the midpoint of MN (IM = IN), then IA = IM = IN = IB. Therefore, IA = IB, so I is the midpoint of AB. \n\nAh! This is the key. \n\nFrom the homothety, we have:\n\nk = IB/IA = IN/IM\n\nBut we already know from power of a point that IM = IN. Therefore:\n\nIB/IA = IN/IM = 1 \u21d2 IB = IA\n\nHence, I is the midpoint of AB. \n\nTherefore, the crucial steps are