 cases:\n\n\\[\n1000 \\text{ (Case 1)} + 1000 \\text{ (Case 2)} = 2000\n\\]\n\nThus, the number of different ways \\(1999^{1999}\\) can be written as the sum of consecutive natural numbers is \\(\\boxed{2000}\\).", "ground_truth_solution": "\nTo determine how many ways \\( 1999^{1999} \\) can be expressed as the sum of consecutive natural numbers, we start with generalizing the problem. If \\( 1999^{1999} \\) can be written as the sum of \\( n \\) consecutive natural numbers starting from \\( a+1 \\), \\( a+2 \\), and so on, then it must satisfy:\n\n1. Let the sum of \\( n \\) consecutive integers starting from \\( a+1 \\) be expressed as:\n    $$\n    (a+1) + (a+2) + \\cdots + (a+n)\n    $$\n   \n2. Using the formula for the sum of an arithmetic series, the above series can be written as:\n    $$\n    (a+1) + (a+2) + \\cdots + (a+n) = n \\cdot a + (1 + 2 + \\cdots + n)\n    $$\n\n3. Applying the formula for the sum of the first \\( n \\) natural numbers \\(\\sum_{k=1}^{n} k = \\frac{n(n+1)}{2}\\):\n    $$\n    n \\cdot a + \\frac{n(n+1)}{2} = 1999^{1999}\n    $$\n\n4. Simplifying the equation:\n    $$\n    \\frac{n(2a + n + 1)}{2} = 1999^{1999}\n    $$\n\n5. Multiplying both sides by 2 to clear the fraction:\n    $$\n    n(2a + n + 1) = 2 \\cdot 1999^{1999}\n    $$\n\n6. Given that 1999 is a prime number, all divisors of \\( 2 \\cdot 1999^{1999} \\) must be of the form \\( 1999^k \\) or \\( 2 \\cdot 1999^k \\) for \\( k = 0, 1, \\ldots, 1999 \\).\n\n### Case Analysis:\n\n- **Case 1: \\( n = 1999^k \\)**\n\n    - Substitute \\( n = 1999^k \\) into the equation:\n        $$\n        1999^k(2a + 1999^k + 1) = 2 \\cdot 1999^{1999}\n        $$\n\n    - Divide both sides by \\( 1999^k \\):\n        $$\n        2a + 1999^k + 1 = 2 \\cdot 1999^{1999-k}\n        $$\n\n    - Rearranging to solve for \\( a \\):\n        $$\n        2a = 2 \\cdot 1999^{1999-k} - 1999^k - 1\n        $$\n\n        $$\n        a = 1999^{1999-k} - \\frac{1999^k + 1}{2}\n        $$\n\n    - Since \