ve group \\((\\mathbb{F}_{p^2}^*, \\cdot)\\) of cardinality \\(p^2 - 1\\), this implies:\n   \\[\n   t \\ \\text{divides} \\ p^2 - 1\n   \\]\n\n#### Step 3: Verification of Period\n\nTo demonstrate that \\(t_p = p^2 - 1\\) is achievable, we use the fact that \\((\\mathbb{F}_{p^2}^*, \\cdot)\\) is cyclic. Thus, there exists an element \\(\\alpha \\in \\mathbb{F}_{p^2}\\) of order \\(p^2 - 1\\). Note that \\(\\alpha \\notin \\mathbb{F}_p\\), as its order does not divide \\(p - 1\\).\n\n1. **Construct the Sequence**\n\n   Define:\n   \\[\n   x_n = \\alpha^n + \\overline{\\alpha}^n\n   \\]\n   where \\(\\overline{\\alpha}\\) is the conjugate of \\(\\alpha\\), which is the other root of its minimal polynomial over \\(\\mathbb{F}_p[X]\\). This sequence fulfills the linear recurrence of order 2, with:\n   \\[\n   t_p \\ \\text{divides} \\ p^2 - 1\n   \\]\n\n2. **Final Check**\n\n   We now need:\n   \\[\n   x_{n+t} \\equiv x_n \\ (\\text{mod} \\ p)\n   \\]\n   This implies:\n   \\[\n   \\alpha^t + \\overline{\\alpha}^t = 2 \\quad \\text{and} \\quad \\alpha^{t+1} + \\overline{\\alpha}^{t+1} = \\alpha + \\overline{\\alpha}\n   \\]\n   Matrix form solution gives:\n   \\[\n   \\begin{cases}\n   x + y = 2 \\\\\n   \\alpha x + \\overline{\\alpha} y = \\alpha + \\overline{\\alpha}\n   \\end{cases}\n   \\]\n   Since \\(\\alpha \\neq \\overline{\\alpha}\\), the determinant \\(\\alpha - \\overline{\\alpha} \\neq 0\\), which implies \\(t = p^2 - 1\\) is correctly the period for some sequences fitting our recurrence relation.\n\n#### Conclusion\n\nHence, the maximum value of \\(t_p\\) is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{p^2 - 1}\n\\]", "domain": "math", "source": "numina_math", "test_cases": null, "starter_code": null}, {"problem": "Consider triangle \\(ABC\\) with angles \\(\\angle BAC = 24^\\circ\\) and \\(\\angle ACB = 28^\\circ\\). Point \\(D\\) is constructed such that \\(AB \\parallel CD\\), \\(AD = BC\\), and \\(AD\\) and \\(BC\\) are not parallel. Similarly, point \\(E\\) is constructed such t