, we need to determine the angle \\(\\angle CPE\\) where points \\(D\\) and \\(E\\) are constructed such that \\(AB \\parallel CD\\), \\(AD = BC\\), \\(AE \\parallel BC\\), \\(AB = CE\\), and lines \\(DE\\) and \\(AC\\) intersect at point \\(P\\).\n\n1. **Angles in Triangle \\(ABC\\)**:\n   - \\(\\angle ABC = 180^\\circ - 24^\\circ - 28^\\circ = 128^\\circ\\).\n\n2. **Constructing Points \\(D\\) and \\(E\\)**:\n   - **Point \\(D\\)**: Constructed such that \\(AB \\parallel CD\\) and \\(AD = BC\\). Using coordinate geometry and vector analysis, point \\(D\\) is found to be approximately \\((-0.123, -0.500)\\).\n   - **Point \\(E\\)**: Constructed such that \\(AE \\parallel BC\\) and \\(AB = CE\\). Using similar methods, point \\(E\\) is found to be approximately \\((1.104, -0.586)\\).\n\n3. **Intersection Point \\(P\\)**:\n   - Line \\(DE\\) is calculated to intersect \\(AC\\) (the x-axis) at point \\(P\\) approximately \\((-7.251, 0)\\).\n\n4. **Calculating \\(\\angle CPE\\)**:\n   - Vectors \\(PC\\) and \\(PE\\) are determined. Vector \\(PC\\) is along the positive x-axis, and vector \\(PE\\) has components calculated from coordinates of \\(E\\) and \\(P\\).\n   - Using the dot product formula and arctangent calculations, the angle between vectors \\(PC\\) and \\(PE\\) is found to be approximately \\(4^\\circ\\).\n\nThus, the measure of angle \\(\\angle CPE\\) is \\(\\boxed{4}\\) degrees.", "ground_truth_solution": "\nWe are given triangle $ABC$ with $\\angle BAC = 24^\\circ$ and $\\angle ACB = 28^\\circ$. We need to determine $\\angle CPE$ where $D$ and $E$ are constructed with specific properties and $DE$ intersects $AC$ at $P$. \n\nStep-by-step solution:\n\n1. **Determine $\\angle ABC$:**\n    - Using the angle sum property of a triangle:\n    \\[\n    \\angle ABC = 180^\\circ - \\angle BAC - \\angle ACB = 180^\\circ - 24^\\circ - 28^\\circ = 128^\\circ\n    \\]\n\n2. **Consider trapezoid $ABCD$:**\n    - Since $AB || CD$ and $AD = BC$, $ABCD$ is an isosceles trapezoid.\n    - Thus, $\\angle BCD = \\angle ABC = 128^\\circ$.\n   \n3. **Consider trapezoid $ABCE$:**\n    - Similarly, $AB || CE$ and $AB = CE$, making $ABCE$ another isosceles trapezoid.\n    - Thus, $\\angle BCE = \\angle ABC = 128^\\circ$.\n   \n4. **Calculate $\\angle ACE$:**\n    - We know $\\angle ACB = 28^\\circ$.\n    - Therefore:\n    \\[\n    \\angle ACE = \\angle BCE - \\angle BCA = 128^\\circ - 28^\\circ = 100^\\circ\n    \\]\n\n5. **Calculate $\\angle CEA$:**\n    - In $\\triangle AEC$:\n    \\[\n    \\angle CEA = 180^\\circ - \\angle ACE = 180^\\circ - 100^\\circ = 80^\\circ\n    \\]\n\n6. **Calculate $\\angle DEA$:**\n    - By the angles within the trapezoid:\n    \\[\n    \\angle DEA = \\angle BAC = 24^\\circ\n    \\]\n\n7. **Calculate $\\angle DEC$:**\n    - $\\angle DEA = 24^\\circ$ and using the properties of internal angles:\n    \\[\n    \\angle DEC = \\angle DEA + \\angle AEC = 24^\\circ + 52^\\circ = 76^\\circ\n    \\]\n\n8. **Consider triangle $CPE$:**\n    - Using the triangle angle sum property:\n    \\[\n    \\angle CPE = 180^\\circ - \\angle AEC - \\angle DEC = 180^\\circ - 100^\\circ - 76^\\circ = 4^\\circ\n    \\]\n\nThus, the measure of $\\angle CPE$ is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{4^\\circ}\n\\]", "domain": "math", "source": "numina_math", "test_cases": null, "starter_code": null}, {"problem": "Petya divided a circle into 7 parts using three lines and wrote 7 different integers in them such that the sums of the numbers on either side of each line were equal. One of the numbers is zero. Prove that one of the numbers is negative.", "deepseek_reasoning": "Okay, so I need to prove that one of the numbers in the circle divided into 7 parts by three lines, with each line having equal sums on both sides, must include a negative number given that one of them is zero. Let me try to break this down step by step.\n\nFirst, let me visualize the problem. A circle divided into 7 parts by three lines. Wait, three lines dividing a circle into 7 parts... Hmm, ho